# Demand, Supply, and Market Equilibrium

> Demand and supply are the twin forces that determine every market price
> and quantity; equilibrium is the point where they finally agree.

[definition]
**Demand** is the quantity of a good buyers are willing *and* able to
purchase at each price over a period. **Supply** is the quantity sellers
are willing and able to offer at each price. Willingness plus ability —
not mere desire — is what counts.
[/definition]

## 1. The Law of Demand

As price rises, quantity demanded falls; as price falls, it rises — an
**inverse relationship**, shown by the downward-sloping demand curve.
The two move together because of the **income effect** (a higher price
cuts real purchasing power) and the **substitution effect** (buyers
switch to cheaper alternatives).

| Price of mango (₹/kg) | Quantity demanded (kg) |
| --- | --- |
| 40 | 5 |
| 30 | 10 |
| 20 | 20 |
| 10 | 35 |

A **demand schedule** is this table; plotting it gives the demand curve.

## 2. Determinants of Demand

Price changes cause a *movement along* the curve. Everything else that
shifts the *whole curve* is a determinant:

- **Income** — normal goods (rise with income) vs inferior goods (fall,
  e.g., coarse cloth as incomes grow).
- **Tastes and preferences** — fashion, advertising, health trends.
- **Prices of related goods** — substitutes (tea and coffee) and
  complements (petrol and cars).
- **Expectations** of future prices — anticipated shortage, buy today.
- **Population** — size and composition (more young families, more
  school-bag demand).

@diagram tree id="demand-determinants" layout="tree" aria="Determinants that shift the demand curve"
  Determinants of Demand -> Income
  Determinants of Demand -> Tastes and Preferences
  Determinants of Demand -> Prices of Related Goods
  Determinants of Demand -> Expectations
  Determinants of Demand -> Population
  "Prices of Related Goods" -> Substitutes
  "Prices of Related Goods" -> Complements
@/diagram

## 3. The Law of Supply

As price rises, quantity supplied rises — a **direct relationship**,
giving an upward-sloping supply curve. Higher prices make production
more profitable, so existing firms produce more and new firms enter.

Key **determinants of supply**: input prices, technology, taxes and
subsidies, prices of other goods, expectations, and the number of
sellers. Better technology or cheaper inputs shift supply rightward.

## 4. Movement Along vs Shift of the Curve

[important title="The most common exam trap"]
A change in the good's **own price** causes a movement *along* a curve.
A change in any *other* factor (income, technology, tastes) shifts the
*entire* curve. "Increase in demand" = curve shifts right; "increase in
quantity demanded" = movement down along the same curve.
[/important]

| Term | What changed | Curve |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Extension of demand | Own price falls | Move down the curve |
| Contraction of demand | Own price rises | Move up the curve |
| Increase in demand | Income, tastes, etc. | Shifts right |
| Decrease in demand | Income, tastes, etc. | Shifts left |

## 5. Equilibrium: Where Curves Cross

@math
Market equilibrium: Q_d = Q_s
@/math

| Price (₹) | Q demanded | Q supplied | Market state | Pressure |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 40 | 5 | 25 | Surplus of 20 | Price falls |
| 30 | 10 | 20 | Surplus of 10 | Price falls |
| 20 | 20 | 20 | **Equilibrium** | None |
| 10 | 35 | 5 | Shortage of 30 | Price rises |

A **surplus** (excess supply) pushes price down; a **shortage**
(excess demand) pushes price up. The market "solves" both on its own —
Adam Smith's invisible hand at work.

## 6. Shifts of Both Curves

If demand rises while supply is fixed, both equilibrium price and
quantity rise. If supply rises while demand is fixed, price falls but
quantity rises. When both shift at once, one outcome becomes ambiguous
(e.g., demand and supply both rise → quantity surely rises, but price
depends on which shift is larger).

[example]
Festival season raises demand for sweets; a poor sugarcane crop cuts
supply. Demand shifts right, supply shifts left — price rises sharply
while quantity change depends on the relative shift sizes.
[/example]

## 7. Exceptions to the Law of Demand

- **Giffen goods** — inferior staples (classic case: bread for poor
  households) where the income effect of a price rise outweighs the
  substitution effect, so quantity demanded *rises* with price.
- **Veblen goods** — ostentatious luxury items (diamonds, designer
  bags) bought *for status*; a higher price increases their appeal.
- **Necessities** — salt, life-saving medicine; quantity barely moves.
- **Speculative demand** — buyers rush in when prices rise, expecting
  further rises (shares, housing booms).

## 8. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | Direction | Trigger |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Law of demand | P↑ → Qd↓ | Own price |
| Law of supply | P↑ → Qs↑ | Own price |
| Rightward demand shift | Demand↑ at every price | Income, tastes, substitutes' prices |
| Rightward supply shift | Supply↑ at every price | Technology, subsidies |
| Surplus | Price falls | P above equilibrium |
| Shortage | Price rises | P below equilibrium |
| Giffen/Veblen goods | Qd rises with price | Income effect / status effect |

[summary]
Prices are signals that ration scarce goods: shortages push prices up,
surpluses push them down, and equilibrium is where neither pressure
exists. Shift the curves, not points, whenever the cause is anything
other than the good's own price.
[/summary]
